WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.960
Next, we're going to talk about classes, objects, and object-oriented programming.

00:04.960 --> 00:10.480
Object-oriented programming, it allows us to represent in a code in a much different way,

00:10.480 --> 00:20.000
organized into classes. The concept of object-oriented programming, it's a language model or a paradigm,

00:20.000 --> 00:27.360
where properties or behaviors are organized into objects. Some programming languages, they

00:28.160 --> 00:34.080
encourage a more procedural style, where you just, you know, like cobalt or basic. You just go through

00:34.080 --> 00:42.000
each thing step by step. You're not jumping to other parts of the program necessarily. But more

00:42.000 --> 00:48.800
modern languages, they tend to adopt a object-oriented style to organize things into objects and provide

00:48.800 --> 00:55.840
methods for those objects to communicate with each other. So what is an object?

00:55.840 --> 01:03.120
It can be, in Python, it can be a function, it could be a variable, a property, it could be a

01:03.120 --> 01:11.360
class. Everything in Python is an object. It's a very important concept. When you think of some

01:11.360 --> 01:17.360
typed languages like Java, it has these, what you can think of primitive types, like an int.

01:17.760 --> 01:25.760
And that is just kind of a very barebow and kind of more down to the level type of type. It doesn't

01:25.760 --> 01:39.440
it's not an object. Whereas in Python, as we saw, even the integers, they're an object. You can

01:39.520 --> 01:50.160
call a method on them. So in Python, everything is an object. You can think of these objects that

01:50.160 --> 01:57.040
are going to make kind of like a generic container, like we can even make our own objects that

01:57.040 --> 02:01.440
maybe look something like a list. To do that, you would have to use magic methods, which I'm

02:01.440 --> 02:09.360
not going to cover today, but something to keep in mind. And if you wanted to make

02:09.360 --> 02:15.120
your object that looks kind of like a list, you might have some helper methods on it. Like

02:17.280 --> 02:22.400
code that might be executed on the whole list, or maybe some of the items on the list, just

02:22.400 --> 02:30.640
to wait to group these concepts together. Python buys pretty heavily into this object oriented

02:30.640 --> 02:36.640
model, but that doesn't mean that you always have to use objects or classes in your programs.

02:36.640 --> 02:41.360
Python also, as we've seen, works perfectly fine in a procedural sort of way.

02:43.120 --> 02:48.160
You know, like a scripting language, where one command is just executed one after the other, like

02:48.160 --> 02:54.880
a recipe. But as you get familiar with object oriented programming, as you read more

02:54.880 --> 03:00.320
Python code written by other people, they'll really learn to help you encapsulate your thinking

03:00.320 --> 03:07.280
and encapsulate your code into objects for better organization and rehability and reuse

03:08.560 --> 03:14.720
and maintainability. Code maintainability is always important. So objects in Python,

03:14.720 --> 03:22.560
there's center stage representing not just the data, but the overall structure of your programs.

